breaking_changes.Adaptation_method:
Update queue method usage to handle the new awaitable behavior:

1. Previous callback approach (still supported):
```javascript
q.drain(function() {
  console.log('all items have been processed');
});

q.error(function(err, task) {
  console.error('task experienced an error');
});
```

2. New promise-based approach:
```javascript
// Using await
await q.drain();

// Or using then/catch
q.drain().then(() => {
  console.log('all items have been processed');
});
```

If your code passes 'false' as an error parameter to callbacks and you want execution to continue, update your code to pass null or undefined instead. For example:

```javascript
// Before - will now cancel waterfall and not call final callback
async.waterfall([
  function(callback) {
    callback(false); // This now cancels the waterfall
  },
  function(callback) {
    // This won't be called in v3
  }
], finalCallback); // finalCallback won't be called

// After - continuation as expected
async.waterfall([
  function(callback) {
    callback(null); // Use null instead of false
  },
  function(callback) {
    // This will be called
  }
], finalCallback); // finalCallback will be called
```

Replace async.during and async.doDuring with the updated methods async.whilst, async.doWhilst, async.until, or async.doUntil, which now accept asynchronous test functions. For example, change:

```javascript
// Old: during with synchronous test
async.during(
  function test() { return count < 5; },
  function iteratee(callback) { /* ... */ },
  function done(err) { /* ... */ }
);

// New: whilst with asynchronous test
async.whilst(
  function test(cb) { cb(null, count < 5); },
  function iteratee(callback) { /* ... */ },
  function done(err) { /* ... */ }
);
```

For doUntil/doWhilst, the test function now receives a callback as its last parameter:

```javascript
// Old: doDuring with synchronous test
async.doDuring(
  function iteratee(callback) { /* ... */ },
  function test(result) { return result < 5; },
  function done(err) { /* ... */ }
);

// New: doWhilst with asynchronous test
async.doWhilst(
  function iteratee(callback) { /* ... */ },
  function test(result, cb) { cb(null, result < 5); },
  function done(err) { /* ... */ }
);
```

If your code relies on memoize to cache error responses (e.g., caching 404 responses), you will need to implement your own error caching mechanism. You can use `async.reflect` in combination with `memoize` to always cache results regardless of whether they were errors: ```javascript
// Before
const memoizedFn = async.memoize(myAsyncFunction);

// After - to maintain error caching behavior
const memoizedFn = async.memoize(async.reflect(myAsyncFunction));
```
